Exactly—and army is more interesting than a single operator because it implies heterogeneous capabilities coordinated toward an objective.

Not just the knight’s move. You want the whole semantic combined-arms formation:

Scouts discover distant relations. Knights flank frames. Engineers build bridges between representations. Translators preserve invariants while changing mediums. Mirrors reverse perspective. Hammers force a common representation for comparison. Dice introduce controlled variation. Judges weight evidence. Archivists preserve fossils. Quartermasters allocate attention and compute. Command selects objectives, formations, and stopping conditions.

And crucially, they shouldn’t all attack the problem simultaneously.

\text{Terrain}
\rightarrow
\text{Metamap}
\rightarrow
\text{Formation}
\rightarrow
\text{Sequence}
\rightarrow
\text{Consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{Evidence}
\rightarrow
\text{Afterglow}
\rightarrow
\text{Metamap update}

Sometimes the right formation isn’t combat at all.

That’s where “lover and fighter” becomes a useful duality rather than contradiction:

\boxed{\text{Connect} \leftrightarrow \text{Contest}}

The fighter asks:

Where is the weakness, contradiction, boundary, or thing that must change?

The lover asks:

What can connect, cooperate, nurture, combine, reconcile, or become mutually beneficial?

Run only Fighter and everything eventually looks adversarial.

Run only Lover and you can miss genuine incompatibilities, exploitation, or constraints.

Run them together and something stronger appears:

\text{Fighter} \rightarrow \textbf{differentiate}

\text{Lover} \rightarrow \textbf{integrate}

Then:

\boxed{
\text{Differentiate}
\rightarrow
\text{Understand}
\rightarrow
\text{Integrate}
\rightarrow
\text{Differentiate again}
}

That’s another oscillating engine for the semantic army.

Know when to separate. Know when to connect. And don’t mistake either operation for the objective itself.